## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical design of data contracts, lineage, and governance in a multi-domain Azure data mesh, with real ingestion patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Data contracts across domains
- End-to-end lineage with Purview
- Delta Lake schema evolution and time travel
- Domain isolation via access controls
- Canary tests for contract changes

## Code Example
```javascript
// pseudo contract test for incoming record
function validateContract(record, contract) {
  // contract = {schema: {...}, required: [...], checks: [...]}
  if (!contract.required.every(k => k in record)) return false;
  // simple type checks
  for (let key of Object.keys(contract.schema)) {
    if (typeof record[key] !== contract.schema[key]) return false;
  }
  // custom checks
  return contract.checks.every(fn => fn(record));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you version contracts and rollback a breaking change?
- How would you monitor contract violations and auto-generate lineage dashboards?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests multi-tenant governance, schema drift handling, lineage, and cost discipline across Azure data services.

## Key Concepts
- Event Hubs ingestion and Spark processing
- Tenant isolation via partitioning and ACLs
- Canonical schema and schema evolution
- Purview for lineage; Delta/Parquet for upserts
- Idempotent loads and per-tenant cost controls

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-Spark ingestion sketch
const df = spark.read.format("eventhubs").options({
  "eventhubs.connectionString": conn,
  "eventhubs.consumerGroup": "$Default"
}).load();

const parsed = df.selectExpr("CAST(body AS STRING) AS json")
  .select(from_json(col("json"), schema).alias("d")).select("d.*");

parsed.write.partitionBy("tenant_id").mode("append").parquet("<out_path>");
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you ensure schema evolution without breaking downstream?
- How would you monitor data lineage and quality across tenants?
